rnates but still shrinks.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the fourth term.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Multiply by −1/3 each time: 18, −6, 2, …","2 × (−1/3)."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":-0.667,"tolerance":0.002},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the sum of the first 4 terms, to 3 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Keep the signs: the odd-powered terms are negative.","Add 18 − 6 + 2 − 2/3."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":13.333,"tolerance":0.02},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why the series converges despite the negative ratio.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["What is |−1/3|?","Does convergence depend on the sign of r?"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["the series converges because the size of the ratio, 1/3, is less than 1; the negative sign only makes the terms alternate in sign"],"keywords":[["series"],["converges"],["size"],["ratio"],["negative"],["sign"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the sum to infinity.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use S∞ = a/(1 − r) with r = −1/3.","The denominator becomes 1 + 1/3 = 4/3."],"strand":"C(iv)","answer":13.5,"tolerance":0.02}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for listing the terms, 1 for the signed sum, 1 for 13.333. (c) 3 marks: 1 for |r|, 1 for the sign''s effect, 1 for the conclusion. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 4/3, 1 for 13.5.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for listing the terms, 1 for the signed sum, 1 for 13.333. (c) 3 marks: 1 for |r|, 1 for the sign''s effect, 1 for the conclusion. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 4/3, 1 for 13.5.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Keep the signs in (b); the alternating terms are what make the estimate in (c) sensible."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for listing the terms, 1 for the signed sum, 1 for 13.333. (c) 3 marks: 1 for |r|, 1 for the sign''s effect, 1 for the conclusion. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 4/3, 1 for 13.5."},{"n":45,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A phone battery loses charge according to B = 100 × 0.92^t, where B is the percentage remaining after t hours of standby.\n\n(a) State the percentage lost each hour.\n(b) Calculate the percentage remaining after 6 hours, to 1 decimal place.\n(c) Using logarithms, calculate the time for the battery to fall to 50%, to 2 decimal places.\n(d) A user claims ''the battery loses 8% of 100 each hour, so it runs out after 12.5 hours''. Evaluate this claim.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A phone battery loses charge according to B = 100 × 0.92^t, where B is the percentage remaining after t hours of standby."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the percentage lost each hour.\n(b) Calculate the percentage remaining after 6 hours, to 1 decimal place.\n(c) Using logarithms, calculate the time for the battery to fall to 50%, to 2 decimal places.\n(d) A user claims ''the battery loses 8% of 100 each hour, so it runs out after 12.5 hours''. Evaluate this claim."}]}]},"e":"0.92 keeps 92%, so 8% of the current charge is lost each hour. B(6) ≈ 60.6% and the 50% level is reached at t = log 0.5 / log 0.92 ≈ 8.31 hours. The 12.5-hour claim wrongly assumes a constant 8 percentage-point loss.","m":10,"df":"hard","tp":"modelling","st":"modelling","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":45,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"An exponential decay loses a constant PERCENTAGE of the current amount, not a constant amount: linear reasoning overestimates the lifetime.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the percentage of charge lost each hour.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The multiplier 0.92 keeps 92%.","100 − 92."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":8,"tolerance":0,"unit":"%"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the percentage remaining after 6 hours, to 1 decimal place.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use 100 × 0.92^6.","0.92^6 ≈ 0.60636."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":60.6,"tolerance":0.1,"unit":"%"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the time to reach 50%, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Solve 0.92^t = 0.5 with logarithms.","t = log 0.5 ÷ log 0.92."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":8.31,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"hours"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Evaluate the claim that the battery runs out after 12.5 hours.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Is 8% of the current charge a constant 8 percentage points?","Compare 12.5 hours with your answer to (c)."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the claim is wrong: it treats the 8% as a constant 8 percentage points, but the model takes 8% of the current charge, so the battery reaches 50% after about 8.31 hours, not 12.5"],"keywords":[["claim"],["wrong"],["treats"],["constant"],["percentage"],["points"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 0.60636, 1 for 60.6%. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 8.31. (d) 3 marks: 1 for identifying the linear error, 1 for the 8.31-hour comparison, 1 for a clear conclusion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 0.60636, 1 for 60.6%. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 8.31. (d) 3 marks: 1 for identifying the linear error, 1 for the 8.31-hour comparison, 1 for a clear conclusion.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot B against t in the workspace and mark the 50% level: the curve, not a straight line, is the counter-argument in (d)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 0.60636, 1 for 60.6%. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 8.31. (